ase change your bookmarks.\par \par \par SAX2 is Free!\par \par I hereby abandon any property rights to SAX 2.0 (the Simple API for\par XML), and release all of the SAX 2.0 source code, compiled code, and\par documentation contained in this distribution into the Public Domain.\par SAX comes with NO WARRANTY or guarantee of fitness for any\par purpose.\par \par David Megginson, david@megginson.com\par 2000-05-05\par \par \par Independent JPEG Group's JPEG software release 6b\par \par The Independent JPEG Group's JPEG software\par ==========================================\par \par README for release 6b of 27-Mar-1998\par ====================================\par \par This distribution contains the sixth public release of the Independent JPEG\par Group's free JPEG software.  You are welcome to redistribute this software and\par to use it for any purpose, subject to the conditions under LEGAL ISSUES, below.\par \par Serious users of this software (particularly those incorporating it into\par larger programs) should contact IJG at jpeg-info@uunet.uu.net to be added to\par our electronic mailing list.  Mailing list members are notified of updates\par and have a chance to participate in technical discussions, etc.\par \par This software is the work of Tom Lane, Philip Gladstone, Jim Boucher,\par Lee Crocker, Julian Minguillon, Luis Ortiz, George Phillips, Davide Rossi,\par Guido Vollbeding, Ge' Weijers, and other members of the Independent JPEG\par Group.\par \par IJG is not affiliated with the official ISO JPEG standards committee.\par \par \par DOCUMENTATION ROADMAP\par =====================\par \par This file contains the following sections:\par \par OVERVIEW            General description of JPEG and the IJG software.\par LEGAL ISSUES        Copyright, lack of warranty, terms of distribution.\par REFERENCES          Where to learn more about JPEG.\par ARCHIVE LOCATIONS   Where to find newer versions of this software.\par RELATED SOFTWARE    Other stuff you should get.\par FILE FORMAT WARS    Software *not* to get.\par TO DO               Plans for future IJG releases.\par \par Other documentation files in the distribution are:\par \par User documentation:\par   install.doc       How to configure and install the IJG software.\par   usage.doc         Usage instructions for cjpeg, djpeg, jpegtran,\par                     rdjpgcom, and wrjpgcom.\par   *.1               Unix-style man pages for programs (same info as usage.doc).\par   wizard.doc        Advanced usage instructions for JPEG wizards only.\par   change.log        Version-to-version change highlights.\par Programmer and internal documentation:\par   libjpeg.doc       How to use the JPEG library in your own programs.\par   example.c         Sample code for calling the JPEG library.\par   structure.doc     Overview of the JPEG library's internal structure.\par   filelist.doc      Road map of IJG files.\par   coderules.doc     Coding style rules --- please read if you contribute code.\par \par Please read at least the files install.doc and usage.doc.  Useful information\par can also be found in the JPEG FAQ (Frequently Asked Questions) article.  See\par ARCHIVE LOCATIONS below to find out where to obtain the FAQ article.\par \par If you want to understand how the JPEG code works, we suggest reading one or\par more of the REFERENCES, then looking at the documentation files (in roughly\par the order listed) before diving into the code.\par \par \par OVERVIEW\par ========\par \par This package contains C software to implement JPEG image compression and\par decompression.  JPEG (pronounced "jay-peg") is a standardized compression\par method for full-color and gray-scale images.  JPEG is intended for compressing\par "real-world" scenes; line drawings, cartoons and other non-realistic images\par are not its strong suit.  JPEG is lossy, meaning that the output image is not\par exactly identical to the input image.  Hence you must not use JPEG if you\par have to have identical output bits.  However, on typical photographic images,\par very good compression levels can be obtained with no visible change, and\par remarkably high compression levels are possible if you can tolerate a\par low-quality image.  For more details, see the references, or just experiment\par with various compression settings.\par \par This software implements JPEG baseline, extended-sequential, and progressive\par compression processes.  Provision is made for supporting all variants of these\par processes, although some uncommon parameter settings aren't implemented yet.\par For legal reasons, we are not distributing code for the arithmetic-coding\par variants of JPEG; see LEGAL ISSUES.  We have made no provision for supporting\par the hierarchical or lossless processes defined in the standard.\par \par We provide a set of library routines for reading and writing JPEG image files,\par plus two sample applications "cjpeg" and "djpeg", which use the library to\par perform conversion between JPEG and some other popular image file formats.\par The library is intended to be reused in other applications.\par \par In order to support file conversion and viewing software, we have included\par considerable functionality beyond the bare JPEG coding/decoding capability;\par for example, the color quantization modules are not strictly part of JPEG\par decoding, but they are essential for output to colormapped file formats or\par colormapped displays.  These extra functions can be compiled out of the\par library if not required for a particular application.  We have also included\par "jpegtran", a utility for lossless transcoding between different JPEG\par processes, and "rdjpgcom" and "wrjpgcom", two simple applications for\par inserting and extracting textual comments in JFIF files.\par \par The emphasis in designing this software has been on achieving portability and\par flexibility, while also making it fast enough to be useful.  In particular,\par the software is not intended to be read as a tutorial on JPEG.  (See the\par REFERENCES section for introductory material.)  Rather, it is intended to\par be reliable, portable, industrial-strength code.  We do not claim to have\par achieved that goal in every aspect of the software, but we strive for it.\par \par We welcome the use of this software as a component of commercial products.\par No royalty is required, but we do ask for an acknowledgement in product\par documentation, as described under LEGAL ISSUES.\par \par \par LEGAL ISSUES\par ============\par \par In plain English:\par \par 1. We don't promise that this software works.  (But if you find any bugs,\par    please let us know!)\par 2. You can use this software for whatever you want.  You don't have to pay us.\par 3. You may not pretend that you wrote this software.  If you use it in a\par    program, you must acknowledge somewhere in your documentation that\par    you've used the IJG code.\par \par In legalese:\par \par The authors make NO WARRANTY or representation, either express or implied,\par with respect to this software, its quality, accuracy, merchantability, or\par fitness for a particular purpose.  This software is provided "AS IS", and you,\par its user, assume the entire risk as to its quality and accuracy.\par \par This software is copyright (C) 1991-1998, Thomas G. Lane.\par All Rights Reserved except as specified below.\par \par Permission is hereby granted to use, copy, modify, and distribute this\par software (or portions thereof) for any purpose, without fee, subject to these\par conditions:\par (1) If any part of the source code for this software is distributed, then this\par README file must be included, with this copyright and no-warranty notice\par unaltered; and any additions, deletions, or changes to the original files\par must be clearly indicated in accompanying documentation.\par (2) If only executable code is distributed, then the accompanying\par documentation must state that "this software is based in part on the work of\par the Independent JPEG Group".\par (3) Permission for use of this software is granted only if the user accepts\par full responsibility for any undesirable consequences; the authors accept\par NO LIABILITY for damages of any kind.\par \par These conditions apply to any software derived from or based on the IJG code,\par not just to the unmodified library.  If you use our work, you ought to\par acknowledge us.\par \par Permission is NOT granted for the use of any IJG author's name or company name\par in advertising or publicity relating to this software or products derived from\par it.  This software may be referred to only as "the Independent JPEG Group's\par software".\par \par We specifically permit and encourage the use of this software as the basis of\par commercial products, provided that all warranty or liability claims are\par assumed by the product vendor.\par \par \par ansi2knr.c is included in this distribution by permission of L. Peter Deutsch,\par sole proprietor of its copyright holder, Aladdin Enterprises of Menlo Park, CA.\par ansi2knr.c is NOT covered by the above copyright and conditions, but instead\par by the usual distribution terms of the Free Software Foundation; principally,\par that you must include source code if you redistribute it.  (See the file\par ansi2knr.c for full details.)  However, since ansi2knr.c is not needed as part\par of any program generated from the IJG code, this does not limit you more than\par the foregoing paragraphs do.\par \par The Unix configuration script "configure" was produced with GNU Autoconf.\par It is copyright by the Free Software Foundation but is freely distributable.\par The same holds for its supporting scripts (config.guess, config.sub,\par ltconfig, ltmain.sh).  Another support script, install-sh, is copyright\par by M.I.T. but is also freely distributable.\par \par It appears that the arithmetic coding option of the JPEG spec is covered by\par patents owned by IBM, AT&T, and Mitsubishi.  Hence arithmetic coding cannot\par legally be used without obtaining one or more licenses.  For this reason,\par support for arithmetic coding has been removed from the free JPEG software.\par (Since arithmetic coding provides only a marginal gain over the unpatented\par Huffman mode, it is unlikely that very many implementations will support it.)\par So far as we are aware, there are no patent restrictions on the remaining\par code.\par \par The IJG distribution formerly included code to read and write GIF files.\par To avoid entanglement with the Unisys LZW patent, GIF reading support has\par been removed altogether, and the GIF writer has been simplified to produce\par "uncompressed GIFs".  This technique does not use the LZW algorithm; the\par resulting GIF files are larger than usual, but are readable by all standard\par GIF decoders.\par \par We are required to state that\par     "The Graphics Interchange Format(c) is the Copyright property of\par     CompuServe Incorporated.  GIF(sm) is a Service Mark property of\par     CompuServe Incorporated."\par \par \par REFERENCES\par ==========\par \par We highly recommend reading one or more of these references before trying to\par understand the innards of the JPEG software.\par \par The best short technical introduction to the JPEG compression algorithm is\par 	Wallace, Gregory K.  "The JPEG Still Picture Compression Standard",\par 	Communications of the ACM, April 1991 (vol. 34 no. 4), pp. 30-44.\par (Adjacent articles in that issue discuss MPEG motion picture compression,\par applications of JPEG, and related topics.)  If you don't have the CACM issue\par handy, a PostScript file containing a revised version of Wallace's article is\par available at ftp://ftp.uu.net/graphics/jpeg/wallace.ps.gz.  The file (actually\par a preprint for an article that appeared in IEEE Trans. Consumer Electronics)\par omits the sample images that appeared in CACM, but it includes corrections\par and some added material.  Note: the Wallace article is copyright ACM and IEEE,\par and it may not be used for commercial purposes.\par \par A somewhat less technical, more leisurely introduction to JPEG can be found in\par "The Data Compression Book" by Mark Nelson and Jean-loup Gailly, published by\par M&T Books (New York), 2nd ed. 1996, ISBN 1-55851-434-1.  This book provides\par good explanations and example C code for a multitude of compression methods\par including JPEG.  It is an excellent source if you are comfortable reading C\par code but don't know much about data compression in general.  The book's JPEG\par sample code is far from industrial-strength, but when you are ready to look\par at a full implementation, you've got one here...\par \par The best full description of JPEG is the textbook "JPEG Still Image Data\par Compression Standard" by William B. Pennebaker and Joan L. Mitchell, published\par by Van Nostrand Reinhold, 1993, ISBN 0-442-01272-1.  Price US$59.95, 638 pp.\par The book includes the complete text of the ISO JPEG standards (DIS 10918-1\par and draft DIS 10918-2).  This is by far the most complete exposition of JPEG\par in existence, and we highly recommend it.\par \par The JPEG standard itself is not available electronically; you must order a\par paper copy through ISO or ITU.  (Unless you feel a need to own a certified\par official copy, we recommend buying the Pennebaker and Mitchell book instead;\par it's much cheaper and includes a great deal of useful explanatory material.)\par In the USA, copies of the standard may be ordered from ANSI Sales at (212)\par 642-4900, or from Global Engineering Documents at (800) 854-7179.  (ANSI\par doesn't take credit card orders, but Global does.)  It's not cheap: as of\par 1992, ANSI was charging $95 for Part 1 and $47 for Part 2, plus 7%\par shipping/handling.  The standard is divided into two parts, Part 1 being the\par actual specification, while Part 2 covers compliance testing methods.  Part 1\par is titled "Digital Compression and Coding of Continuous-tone Still Images,\par Part 1: Requirements and guidelines" and has document numbers ISO/IEC IS\par 10918-1, ITU-T T.81.  Part 2 is titled "Digital Compression and Coding of\par Continuous-tone Still Images, Part 2: Compliance testing" and has document\par numbers ISO/IEC IS 10918-2, ITU-T T.83.\par \par Some extensions to the original JPEG standard are defined in JPEG Part 3,\par a newer ISO standard numbered ISO/IEC IS 10918-3 and ITU-T T.84.  IJG\par currently does not support any Part 3 extensions.\par \par The JPEG standard does not specify all details of an interchangeable file\par format.  For the omitted details we follow the "JFIF" conventions, revision\par 1.02.  A copy of the JFIF spec is available from:\par 	Literature Department\par 	C-Cube Microsystems, Inc.\par 	1778 McCarthy Blvd.\par 	Milpitas, CA 95035\par 	phone (408) 944-6300,  fax (408) 944-6314\par A PostScript version of this document is available by FTP at\par ftp://ftp.uu.net/graphics/jpeg/jfif.ps.gz.  There is also a plain text\par version at ftp://ftp.uu.net/graphics/jpeg/jfif.txt.gz, but it is missing\par the figures.\par \par The TIFF 6.0 file format specification can be obtained by FTP from\par ftp://ftp.sgi.com/graphics/tiff/TIFF6.ps.gz.  The JPEG incorporation scheme\par found in the TIFF 6.0 spec of 3-June-92 has a number of serious problems.\par IJG does not recommend use of the TIFF 6.0 design (TIFF Compression tag 6).\par Instead, we recommend the JPEG design proposed by TIFF Technical Note #2\par (Compression tag 7).  Copies of this Note can be obtained from ftp.sgi.com or\par from ftp://ftp.uu.net/graphics/jpeg/.  It is expected that the next revision\par of the TIFF spec will replace the 6.0 JPEG design with the Note's design.\par Although IJG's own code does not support TIFF/JPEG, the free libtiff library\par uses our library to implement TIFF/JPEG per the Note.  libtiff is available\par from ftp://ftp.sgi.com/graphics/tiff/.\par \par \par ARCHIVE LOCATIONS\par =================\par \par The "official" archive site for this software is ftp.uu.net (Internet\par address 192.48.96.9).  The most recent released version can always be found\par there in directory graphics/jpeg.  This particular version will be archived\par as ftp://ftp.uu.net/graphics/jpeg/jpegsrc.v6b.tar.gz.  If you don't have\par direct Internet access, UUNET's archives are also available via UUCP; contact\par help@uunet.uu.net for information on retrieving files that way.\par \par Numerous Internet sites maintain copies of the UUNET files.  However, only\par ftp.uu.net is guaranteed to have the latest official version.\par \par You can also obtain this software in DOS-compatible "zip" archive format from\par the SimTel archives (ftp://ftp.simtel.net/pub/simtelnet/msdos/graphics/), or\par on CompuServe in the Graphics Support forum (GO CIS:GRAPHSUP), library 12\par "JPEG Tools".  Again, these versions may sometimes lag behind the ftp.uu.net\par release.\par \par The JPEG FAQ (Frequently Asked Questions) article is a useful source of\par general information about JPEG.  It is updated constantly and therefore is\par not included in this distribution.  The FAQ is posted every two weeks to\par Usenet newsgroups comp.graphics.misc, news.answers, and other groups.\par It is available on the World Wide Web at http://www.faqs.org/faqs/jpeg-faq/\par and other news.answers archive sites, including the official news.answers\par archive at rtfm.mit.edu: ftp://rtfm.mit.edu/pub/usenet/news.answers/jpeg-faq/.\par If you don't have Web or FTP access, send e-mail to mail-server@rtfm.mit.edu\par with body\par 	send usenet/news.answers/jpeg-faq/part1\par 	send usenet/news.answers/jpeg-faq/part2\par \par \par RELATED SOFTWARE\par ================\par \par Numerous viewing and image manipulation programs now support JPEG.  (Quite a\par few of them use this library to do so.)  The JPEG FAQ described above lists\par some of the more popular free and shareware viewers, and tells where to\par obtain them on Internet.\par \par If you are on a Unix machine, we highly recommend Jef Poskanzer's free\par PBMPLUS software, which provides many useful operations on PPM-format image\par files.  In particular, it can convert PPM images to and from a wide range of\par other formats, thus making cjpeg/djpeg considerably more useful.  The latest\par version is distributed by the NetPBM group, and is available from numerous\par sites, notably ftp://wuarchive.wustl.edu/graphics/graphics/packages/NetPBM/.\par Unfortunately PBMPLUS/NETPBM is not nearly as portable as the IJG software is;\par you are likely to have difficulty making it work on any non-Unix machine.\par \par A different free JPEG implementation, written by the PVRG group at Stanford,\par is available from ftp://havefun.stanford.edu/pub/jpeg/.  This program\par is designed for research and experimentation rather than production use;\par it is slower, harder to use, and less portable than the IJG code, but it\par is easier to read and modify.  Also, the PVRG code supports lossless JPEG,\par which we do not.  (On the other hand, it doesn't do progressive JPEG.)\par \par \par FILE FORMAT WARS\par ================\par \par Some JPEG programs produce files that are not compatible with our library.\par The root of the problem is that the ISO JPEG committee failed to specify a\par concrete file format.  Some vendors "filled in the blanks" on their own,\par creating proprietary formats that no one else could read.  (For example, none\par of the early commercial JPEG implementations for the Macintosh were able to\par exchange compressed files.)\par \par The file format we have adopted is called JFIF (see REFERENCES).  This format\par has been agreed to by a number of major commercial JPEG vendors, and it has\par become the de facto standard.  JFIF is a minimal or "low end" representation.\par We recommend the use of TIFF/JPEG (TIFF revision 6.0 as modified by TIFF\par Technical Note #2) for "high end" applications that need to record a lot of\par additional data about an image.  TIFF/JPEG is fairly new and not yet widely\par supported, unfortunately.\par \par The upcoming JPEG Part 3 standard defines a file format called SPIFF.\par SPIFF is interoperable with JFIF, in the sense that most JFIF decoders should\par be able to read the most common variant of SPIFF.  SPIFF has some technical\par advantages over JFIF, but its major claim to fame is simply that it is an\par official standard rather than an informal one.  At this point it is unclear\par whether SPIFF will supersede JFIF or whether JFIF will remain the de-facto\par standard.  IJG intends to support SPIFF once the standard is frozen, but we\par have not decided whether it should become our default output format or not.\par (In any case, our decoder will remain capable of reading JFIF indefinitely.)\par \par Various proprietary file formats incorporating JPEG compression also exist.\par We have little or no sympathy for the existence of these formats.  Indeed,\par one of the original reasons for developing this free software was to help\par force convergence on common, open format standards for JPEG files.  Don't\par use a proprietary file format!\par \par The major thrust for v7 will probably be improvement of visual quality.\par The current method for scaling the quantization tables is known not to be\par very good at low Q values.  We also intend to investigate block boundary\par smoothing, "poor man's variable quantization", and other means of improving\par quality-vs-file-size performance without sacrificing compatibility.\par \par In future versions, we are considering supporting some of the upcoming JPEG\par Part 3 extensions --- principally, variable quantization and the SPIFF file\par format.\par \par As always, speeding things up is of great interest.\par \par Please send bug reports, offers of help, etc. to jpeg-info@uunet.uu.net.\par \par PuTTY 0.58 (derivative work)\par Portions of the SWT